  # Header Kicker
  - elementId: kicker
    elementType: text
    bounds: [50, 26, 600, 16]
    content:
      style: "$kicker"
      text: "05 · SUPPLY-CHAIN BOTTLENECKS"

  # Header Title
  - elementId: title
    elementType: text
    bounds: [50, 42, 860, 30]
    content:
      style: "$title"
      text: "Global Civil Tritium Inventory Exhausts by 2038; Commercial DT Fusion Must Close the Fuel Loop"

  # Header Subtitle
  - elementId: subtitle
    elementType: text
    bounds: [50, 72, 860, 16]
    content:
      style: "$subtitle"
      text: "The civil tritium inventory cliff, neutron economy physics, and radiation damage limits on first-wall plasma-facing components"

  # Header Divider
  - elementId: header-line
    elementType: line
    bounds: [50, 92, 860, 1]
    points: "0,0 860,0"
    border: {style: solid, width: 0.8, color: "$border"}

  # Panel 1: Tritium Breeding Loop
  - elementId: p1-bg
    elementType: shape
    bounds: [50, 106, 420, 268]
    shapeName: rect
    fill: {type: solid, color: "$cardBg"}
    border: {style: solid, width: 0.8, color: "$border"}

  - elementId: p1-header
    elementType: shape
    bounds: [50, 106, 420, 28]
    shapeName: rect
    fill: {type: solid, color: "$titleNavy"}

  - elementId: p1-title
    elementType: text
    bounds: [60, 111, 400, 18]
    content:
      fontSize: 9
      bold: true
      color: "$white"
      text: "THE CIVIL TRITIUM CLIFF & BREEDING RATIO (TBR)"

  - elementId: p1-content
    elementType: text
    bounds: [60, 142, 400, 216]
    content:
      fontSize: 8.2
      color: "$ink"
      lineHeight: 1.35
      text: |
        <p><b>Civil Inventory Exhaustion Dynamics:</b></p>
        <p>• Tritium decays radioactively with a half-life of 12.3 years (5.47% annual loss).</p>
        <p>• Civilian supply depends exclusively on Canadian CANDU heavy water reactors (~25–30 kg peak inventory in 2025).</p>
        <p>• CANDU fleet retirements begin 2028–2035; global civil inventory falls below 10 kg by 2035, while ITER alone requires ~12 kg for its test campaigns.</p>
        <p><b>Mandatory TBR Target:</b> \( \mathrm{TBR} = \frac{\text{Tritium Bred}}{\text{Tritium Burned}} \ge 1.05 - 1.15 \)</p>
        <p>A margin of +5% to +15% is required to offset radioactive decay, fuel processing losses, and core inventory buildup.</p>
        <p><br><b>Blanket Chemistry &amp; Multipliers:</b></p>
        <p>• ⁶Li + n → ⁴He + T + 4.78 MeV (thermal neutrons)</p>
        <p>• ⁷Li + n → ⁴He + T + n' - 2.47 MeV (fast neutrons)</p>
        <p>• Liquid Lithium-Lead (PbLi) or ceramic pebble beds (Li₄SiO₄) with Beryllium/Lead multipliers.</p>

  # Panel 2: 14 MeV First-Wall Materials
  - elementId: p2-bg
    elementType: shape
    bounds: [490, 106, 420, 268]
    shapeName: rect
    fill: {type: solid, color: "$cardBg"}
    border: {style: solid, width: 0.8, color: "$border"}

  - elementId: p2-header
    elementType: shape
    bounds: [490, 106, 420, 28]
    shapeName: rect
    fill: {type: solid, color: "$accentBlue"}

  - elementId: p2-title
    elementType: text
    bounds: [500, 111, 400, 18]
    content:
      fontSize: 9
      bold: true
      color: "$white"
      text: "FIRST-WALL 14.1 MeV NEUTRON EMBRITTLEMENT"

  - elementId: p2-content
    elementType: text
    bounds: [500, 142, 400, 216]
    content:
      fontSize: 8.2
      color: "$ink"
      lineHeight: 1.35
      text: |
        <p><b>14.1 MeV Neutron Hardness vs Fission:</b></p>
        <p>• Fission neutrons average 1–2 MeV; 14.1 MeV fusion neutrons exceed the threshold for nuclear transmutation reactions: \( (n, \alpha) \) and \( (n, p) \).</p>
        <p>• Transmutation produces helium and hydrogen atoms trapped in the metallic crystal lattice, causing rapid void swelling, hardening, and grain boundary embrittlement.</p>
        <p><br><b>Structural Damage Rates:</b></p>
        <p>• First-wall steels endure <b>10–15 dpa/year</b> (displacements per atom) at 1–2 MW/m² neutron wall loading.</p>
        <p>• Standard austenitic steels fail after &lt;10 dpa; commercial plants require materials enduring &gt;50–100 dpa.</p>
        <p><br><b>Advanced Material Candidates &amp; Testing Void:</b></p>
        <p>• Reduced Activation Ferritic-Martensitic (RAFM) steels (Eurofer97, CLF-1), Oxide Dispersion Strengthened (ODS) alloys, and SiC/SiC composites.</p>
        <p>• <b>Testing Gap:</b> No high-flux 14 MeV neutron source exists worldwide. IFMIF-DONES in Spain is delayed to the 2030s, leaving material certification unhedged.</p>

